/target
/esp
/.qemu
*.log
*.fd

# Model weights and converter artifacts: fetched or generated, never authored.
# The scripts that produce them ARE tracked -- tools/convert.py and
# tools/tokenizer.py -- so all of it is reproducible from the repo plus a
# download. `/tools` used to be ignored wholesale, from when it was scratch
# space, which would have silently dropped both converters.
esp/GLADOS/
tools/venv/
tools/hf/
tools/qwen3/
tools/out/
# The GitHub CLI, downloaded rather than installed system-wide. A tool, not
# a dependency: nothing in the tree calls it and a checkout without it works.
tools/gh/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

out/*

# **Except the release notes, and the reason is the `cuda/` one above: a file
# the release depends on cannot be a file the tree ignores.** `release.yml`
# creates the GitHub release and had nothing to put in it, so it hardcoded
# "Notes written by hand" -- and `tools/site.py` builds the front page's news
# column from the release *body*, so the placeholder reached the site as well.
# The notes were always written. They were written *here*, inside an ignored
# directory, so CI could not read the one file it needed and every release
# waited on somebody remembering to paste them in. 1.3.8 shipped with the
# placeholder for exactly that reason.
#
# Only the prose is re-included; the ISOs, the .efi and everything else beside
# it stay ignored, which is what `out/` is for. Git cannot re-include a file
# under an excluded *directory*, hence the four lines rather than one negation.
!out/release/
out/release/*
!out/release/NOTES-*.md
!out/release/CHANGELOG-*.md

# Built images. Large, reproducible from the repo plus a model, and
# distributed through Releases rather than through git.
dist/

# The update signing key. Its private half must never reach the repository:
# a signer whose private half has been published is not a signer, and rotating
# it needs a kernel update signed by the old one.
update.key
*.key

# Supabase CLI scratch, rewritten on every invocation.
supabase/.temp/

# The pool is its own workspace, so its build directory is its own too.
# `/target` above is anchored at the repository root and does not reach it.
pool/target

# The miner is its own workspace, and nvcc build products are artefacts.
#
# `cuda/` was ignored wholesale until now, on the reason that the GPU work
# lived only on exp/xpu and checking that branch out must not leave this one
# dirty. That stopped being true when `miner/` started needing `cuda/xpu.cu`
# to build: a source the tree depends on cannot be a file the tree ignores.
# Only the artefacts nvcc emits are ignored now.
miner/target
cuda/*.exe
cuda/*.exp
cuda/*.lib
cuda/*.pdb
out/ci-fixture*

# DOOM: the port was an experiment in whether software written elsewhere
# survives being brought over. It answered that, it was never finished, and
# it is not part of this system. The tree stays on disk for anyone who wants
# to read it; nothing builds it and nothing tracks it.
src/doom/
tools/doominfo.py
tools/mkwad.py

# The author's compile gate writes this and removes it; ignored so a
# crash between the two cannot leave it somewhere a `git add` would find.
/.authorcheck.patch
